WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.447
Picture a small restaurant run the old way, from lesson 1.

2
00:00:06.447 --> 00:00:10.064
Each waiter stays with one table until its meal is done.

3
00:00:10.064 --> 00:00:13.294
It has 10 waiters, and the kitchen has 10 burners.

4
00:00:13.294 --> 00:00:19.367
Nobody planned it, but with 10 waiters, no more than 10 orders ever reach the burners at once.

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.223
Our shop has a service like that.

6
00:00:22.223 --> 00:00:27.693
Before every checkout, it works out what the order costs in the customer's own money.

7
00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:35.864
It looks up the customer's level in the shop's database: 5 milliseconds on a borrowed connection, an open line to the database.

8
00:00:35.864 --> 00:00:41.076
Then it asks lesson 4's exchange-rate service for today's rate: 100 milliseconds.

9
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.847
The database has a connection pool: 10 connections that requests borrow and give back.

10
00:00:46.847 --> 00:00:51.903
A request that finds all 10 in use waits, and gives up after 30 milliseconds.

11
00:00:51.903 --> 00:00:57.156
The service itself runs on a fixed pool of 10 platform threads, the costly kind.

12
00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:03.926
Now the move to virtual threads looks like one line: swap the fixed pool for a virtual thread per request.

13
00:01:03.926 --> 00:01:06.251
Every test with light traffic passes.

14
00:01:06.251 --> 00:01:11.025
So here's the question we're shy to ask: what could one line possibly break?

15
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:19.110
The lesson's MigrationDemo sends a burst of 300 requests at once.

16
00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.766
Line A is the old pool: 3,200 milliseconds, all 300 fine.

17
00:01:23.766 --> 00:01:27.197
That's 30 rounds of 10, at about 105 each.

18
00:01:27.197 --> 00:01:29.893
Now line B, with virtual threads.

19
00:01:29.893 --> 00:01:32.180
How fast, and how many fine?

20
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:38.148
173 milliseconds, and only 63 fine.

21
00:01:38.148 --> 00:01:43.421
The other 237 failed: no connection after 30 milliseconds.

22
00:01:43.421 --> 00:01:44.603
Four in five.

23
00:01:44.603 --> 00:01:48.967
It's the fastest way yet to say no to customers.

24
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.143
Back to the restaurant.

25
00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:55.096
The owner moves to order slips, cheap enough for one per table.

26
00:01:55.096 --> 00:01:56.979
Drinks and bread come at once.

27
00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:02.752
But at the first rush, 300 slips reach the kitchen together, and there are still 10 burners.

28
00:02:02.752 --> 00:02:05.136
Most orders come back with an apology.

29
00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:06.138
Let's map it.

30
00:02:06.138 --> 00:02:08.753
The 10 waiters were the pool's threads.

31
00:02:08.753 --> 00:02:11.703
The burners are the 10 database connections.

32
00:02:11.703 --> 00:02:17.134
The slips are virtual threads, and nothing stops 300 of them reaching 10 burners.

33
00:02:17.134 --> 00:02:21.961
A pool tuned for 10 callers gave the rest 30 milliseconds, then said no.

34
00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:24.581
So the old pool did two jobs.

35
00:02:24.581 --> 00:02:28.541
It ran the requests, and it kept the database to 10 callers.

36
00:02:28.541 --> 00:02:30.720
Nobody wrote the second job down.

37
00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.340
Those 10 waiters did crowd control for years, and nobody thanked them.

38
00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.895
Virtual threads take over the first job, and quietly drop the second.

39
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.452
So write the second job back, on purpose, around the one call that needs it.

40
00:02:45.452 --> 00:02:52.488
It's lesson 3's grill tokens: 10 tokens by the burners, one per connection, and the slips wait on the board.

41
00:02:52.488 --> 00:02:58.483
In Java, that's a Semaphore of 10, a counter of permits, held only around the database call.

42
00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.199
Line C runs that version, with the same 300 requests.

43
00:03:03.199 --> 00:03:05.613
How fast now, and how many fine?

44
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.635
283 milliseconds, all 300 fine.

45
00:03:10.635 --> 00:03:14.674
Eleven times faster than the old pool, with no failures.

46
00:03:14.674 --> 00:03:24.697
The requests still met the database 10 at a time, but they waited at the semaphore, where waiting is cheap and has no 30 millisecond limit.

47
00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.783
And the rate calls, which never needed a limit, went from 10 at a time to 180.

48
00:03:29.783 --> 00:03:32.849
So credit where it's due: the old pool was safe.

49
00:03:32.849 --> 00:03:37.065
It was just slow, because it held back calls that needed no limit.

50
00:03:37.767 --> 00:03:41.019
Why not give the database 300 connections instead?

51
00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:45.506
Because a database can run only a few queries truly at the same time.

52
00:03:45.506 --> 00:03:49.279
Past that, more connections add work inside it, not speed.

53
00:03:49.279 --> 00:03:52.791
You don't add burners because you hired cheap waiters.

54
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.738
So how do you see where requests wait?

55
00:03:55.738 --> 00:03:59.742
Not with the thread count: fifty thousand virtual threads is normal.

56
00:03:59.742 --> 00:04:03.333
Watch the requests in flight, and the line at each semaphore.

57
00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:13.752
Little's law ties them together: requests in flight equal throughput, the requests finished per second, times the time each one spends inside.

58
00:04:13.752 --> 00:04:24.110
The lesson's MeasureDemo sends 400 a second, and each spends about 0.12 seconds inside: 20 milliseconds on the database, then 100 for the rate.

59
00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.428
So about 48 should be in flight.

60
00:04:26.428 --> 00:04:27.515
It measured 47.

61
00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:30.727
Turn it around to find the bottleneck.

62
00:04:30.727 --> 00:04:38.787
10 permits, each held for 20 milliseconds, serve at most 10 divided by 0.02: 500 requests a second.

63
00:04:38.787 --> 00:04:41.392
So what happens at 600 a second?

64
00:04:44.867 --> 00:04:50.164
The line at the semaphore grew on every sample: 45, 104, 169, 221.

65
00:04:50.164 --> 00:04:54.659
Throughput stayed at 462 a second, however many arrived.

66
00:04:54.659 --> 00:05:00.359
A rising queue with flat throughput is what a full resource looks like.

67
00:05:00.359 --> 00:05:03.650
The thread count would have said nothing.

68
00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:05.977
The queue named the database.

69
00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:15.239
Here's the fix in Java and Kotlin: acquire on the line before try, and release in finally, around the database call only.

70
00:05:15.239 --> 00:05:18.909
Kotlin wraps that in a small function, withDbPermit.

71
00:05:18.909 --> 00:05:20.885
The rate call stays outside.

72
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.529
So, what could one line break?

73
00:05:23.529 --> 00:05:25.201
A limit nobody wrote down.

74
00:05:25.201 --> 00:05:31.890
Removing a pool removes a limit, so write it back: one semaphore per resource, sized from that resource.

75
00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:35.877
Then count where requests wait, not how many threads you have.

76
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:36.857
Quick check.

77
00:05:36.857 --> 00:05:40.998
To go faster, someone raises the semaphore to 300 permits.

78
00:05:40.998 --> 00:05:43.712
The database still has 10 connections.

79
00:05:43.712 --> 00:05:45.925
What happens in the next burst?

80
00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:50.625
The limit is gone again.

81
00:05:50.625 --> 00:05:56.231
300 callers reach 10 connections, and most of them time out, just like line B.
